cartridge "PENGUINS/AHOY! OCT 1986/1986"

' PENGUINS
' A ColecoVision game based on "Penguins", Ahoy! October 1986 (Commodore
' 64, machine code; the disk and the program name no author). Made for Amy
' Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a byte-for-byte port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/penguins.dis), the game is rebuilt for the
' ColecoVision with new pictures, smooth sprites, ice floes drawn as tiles
' that drift two pixels at a time, and new sounds.
'
' The game: lead your penguin from the river bank at the bottom to the
' burrows in the cliff at the top. Two lanes of deep water, an ice shelf
' with a seal on it, two lanes of shallow water: sharks swim the lanes, ice
' floes drift with them. The penguin never stops by itself: it keeps going
' the way the stick last pointed until a button stops it. In open water the
' current carries it up a pixel a frame; on a floe it rests. A shark, the
' seal or the bare cliff wall costs a penguin (three penguins). A burrow
' pays 100 points for every unit left on the time bar, and the river runs
' faster after each one.
'
' From the original (orig/): the screen's lanes (deep water: sharks and
' floes to the right at 2 pixels a step, to the left at 1; ice shelf: a
' seal to the left at half that; shallow water: one lane to the right, one
' to the left at 1); a step every (15 - homes) / 4 frames, faster with each
' burrow reached; the penguin moving 1 pixel a frame in the latched stick
' direction, the button (or a diagonal) stopping it; the up current in open
' water, cancelled by touching a floe; death on a shark, the seal, or the
' cliff between the burrows; the time bar of 39 units, one lost every 25
' frames, the bonus 100 x time left (nothing at zero, and no death); a new
' life starts with full time, facing down at the bottom centre; three
' penguins; a high score.
'
' Liberties: the screen is redrawn for 256 x 192 (three burrows four
' columns wide, lanes two rows high); two sharks a lane in deep water and
' one in shallow water (the C64 had three and one or two in a 512-pixel
' loop, the ColecoVision lane is 256 pixels), so that the penguin's two
' sprites and a lane's sharks stay within four sprites a line; floes are
' tiles; the river's speed-up stops after 9 burrows (the C64 sped up to
' 8 pixels a frame, then wrapped round to very slow after 15); a burrow
' that is already home to a penguin is closed (it kills, as the wall does),
' and when all three are full they empty for a 1000-point bonus (on the
' C64 any burrow could be used again and again, so holding the stick up
' from the start scored for ever); a diagonal keeps the old direction
' instead of stopping; sharks appear and vanish at columns 2 and 28 (TV
' overscan); collisions are boxes, not the C64's pixel collisions; the
' C64's in-game melody is replaced by a soft paddle sound while swimming;
' the title tune is new; F1 to restart is a button.
'
' Controls: joystick only (either controller). The stick sets the
' penguin's direction; a button stops it. A button starts the game on the
' title.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`.

